   "id": "k2-52",
   "name": "Reversible reactions: heating hydrated copper(II) sulphate"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A reversible reaction can go both ways: the products can change back into the reactants. We write it with a double arrow ⇌.\nHeating blue hydrated copper(II) sulphate gives white anhydrous copper(II) sulphate and water (steam).\nAdding water back to the white powder turns it blue again, and heat is given out (it gets warm).\nCuSO₄·5H₂O ⇌ CuSO₄ + 5H₂O\nThis is used as a test for water: white anhydrous copper(II) sulphate turns blue with water. (Cobalt chloride paper turns from blue to pink with water.)\nMost burning reactions are not reversible: ash cannot become wood again.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "A few drops of a colourless liquid are added to white anhydrous copper(II) sulphate. It turns blue. What does this show?",
  "steps": [
   "White anhydrous copper(II) sulphate turns blue when water is added.",
   "So the liquid contains water."
  ],
  "answer": "The liquid contains water.",
  "checks": []
 },
